speechtext:
You will be better satisfied if you let me finish reading this. The next paragraphs read:
(d)	to sell or otherwise dispose of any agricultural product purchased by the board;
<e) to package, process, store, ship, transport, export or insure any agricultural product;
(f)	to enter into contracts or appoint agents to do anything authorized under this act;
(g)	to purchase at market or contract prices and export any agricultural product under any contract between His Majesty in right of Canada and any other government or agency thereof and to do all things necessarily incidental thereto;
(h)	to purchase at the request of any department of the government of Canada any agricultural product required by such department;
(i)	to appoint commodity boards or other agents to undertake the purchase and the disposition of agricultural products;
(j)	to appoint a committee or committees to assist the beard in an advisory capacity.
Those are all the different paragraphs of subsection 1 and they indicate what the government can do about prices. Then when we come to deal with the question as to whether we should have a formula to indicate what parity is and operate under that, that is dealt with in subsection 2 in this way:
(2) In prescribing prices under paragraphs (a) and (c) of subsection one of this section, the board shall endeavour to ensure adequate and stable returns for agriculture by promoting orderly adjustment from war to peace conditions and shall endeavour to secure a fair relationship between the returns from agriculture and those from other occupations.
That does not use the word "parity", but it sets out in clear language that costs are to be taken into consideration when the price offered by the government is set. We took into consideration the cost of producing butter when we said that we are prepared to pay 58 cents. We took into consideration the cost of producing other products when we suggested that we were prepared to buy them at particular prices. I think the results that we have obtained in that regard all along the line have been fairly satisfactory. In the case of butter, we started out 
at 58 cents and we operated for one year. As I remember, nearly every hon. member who spoke in this house criticized us because we were getting too much butter. They said that we would not be able to sell it. Then we out it down 5 cents and now they tell us we did not get enough butter.
